> gcd of x^2-1 and x^2+2x+1
  ucas: gcd x^2-1, x^2+2x+1
gcd x^2 - 1, x^2 + 2x + 1
  = x + 1
  check: it divides both exactly, and the cofactors are coprime
  (normalized: integer coefficients without a common factor, leading coefficient positive)
> the discriminant of a x^2 + b x + c
  ucas: discriminant a x^2 + b x + c
discriminant a*x^2 + b*x + c
  in x (degree 2): b^2 - 4a*c
  check: at sample values of the coefficients the numeric polynomial's discriminant is -0.7279, the formula gives -0.7279 (agrees)
> what is the remainder when x^3 + 1 is divided by x + 2?
  ucas: rem x^3 + 1, x + 2
rem x^3 + 1, x + 2
  = -7
  (in x: P = Q*quotient + remainder, deg remainder < 1)
  check: exact (both sides have the same normal form)
> plug in x = 2 into x^2 + 1
  ucas: substitute x = 2 in x^2 + 1
x^2 + 1 with x = 2
  = 5
  check: exact (both sides have the same normal form)
> what is the integral of 1/(x^3 + 1)?
  ucas: it(x) = 1/(x^3 + 1)
  ucas: integrate it
∫ 1/(x^3 + 1) dx
  = log(abs(x + 1))/3 - log(x^2 - x + 1)/6 + atan((2x - 1)/sqrt(3))/sqrt(3) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 400 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 1.3e-15)
